 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to authorizing the city school districts of Cohoes and Watervliet to require minors five years of age to attend kindergarten",
    "basePrintNo": "S2274",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S2274-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3205, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S2274\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the education law, in relation to\nauthorizing the city school districts of Cohoes and Watervliet to\nrequire minors five years of age to attend kindergarten\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nAuthorizes the Cohoes and Watervliet city school districts to require\nminors five years of age to attend kindergarten\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 gives authorization to the Cohoes city school district and\nWatervliet city school district to make kindergarten compulsory for\nall five year olds, except for those children whose parents choose not\nto enroll them until the following September.\n\nSection 2 sets forth the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nKindergarten is one of the many components of early intervention that\nour education system uses to get students on the right track and\nprepared for the rest of their education. The City of Cohoes School\nDistrict and the City of Watervliet School District provide full day\nkindergarten programs to help children receive the educational\nbenefits and longer term successes that early education provides.\n\nUnfortunately, many children of kindergarten age are absent from\nschool because their attendance is not required. The Cohoes and\nWatervliet School Boards passed resolutions emphasizing the need for\nquality early education and the positive impact required kindergarten\nattendance would have on the children in the community. This measure\nis designed to address the kindergarten attendance issue in the Cohoes\nCity School District requiring five-year-old children to benefit from\nthe District's kindergarten initiative for long-term academic success.\nCurrently, Cohoes has an average absentee rate for kindergarten\nstudents of 26% between its three elementary schools.  Although not as\nsevere, Watervliet is facing a 5% absentee rate, which is still\nimpacting its students.\n\nA similar provision in Education Law was adopted for the Syracuse City\nSchool District over 25 years ago and recently for the Rochester, New\nYork City, Buffalo, and Utica School Districts.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNone.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
